{"name":"Photon Projector","hex":"#88eeff","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"photon","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The quantum of light and other electromagnetic energy, regarded as a discrete particle having zero rest mass, no electric charge, and an indefinitely long lifetime. It is a gauge boson."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/photon"},{"term":"projector","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Someone who devises or suggests a project; a proposer or planner of something. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","An optical device that projects a beam of light, especially one used to project an image (or moving images) onto a screen."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/projector"}],"entries":[{"title":"Photon","blocks":[{"text":"A photon is an elementary particle that is a quantum of the electromagnetic field, including electromagnetic radiation such as light and radio waves, and the force carrier for the electromagnetic force. Photons are massless particles that can only move at one speed, the speed of light measured in a vacuum. The photon belongs to the class of boson particles."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Photon","wikidata":"Q3198"},{"title":"Projector","blocks":[{"text":"A projector or image projector is an optical device that projects an image onto a surface, commonly a projection screen. Most projectors create an image by shining a light through a small transparent lens, but some newer types of projectors can project the image directly, by using lasers. A virtual retinal display, or retinal projector, is a projector that projects an image directly on the retina instead of using an external projection screen."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/IFA_2012_IMG_5767.JPG/330px-IFA_2012_IMG_5767.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Projector","wikidata":"Q311666"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Young_Diffraction.png/500px-Young_Diffraction.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Young_Diffraction.png/1280px-Young_Diffraction.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Young_Diffraction.png/500px-Young_Diffraction.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Thomas Young's sketch of interference based on observations of water waves.[35] Young reasoned that the similar effects observed with light supported a wave model and not Newton's particle theory of light.[20]: 964 ","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AYoung_Diffraction.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Stimulatedemission.png/500px-Stimulatedemission.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Stimulatedemission.png/500px-Stimulatedemission.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Stimulated emission (in which photons \"clone\" themselves) was predicted by Einstein in his kinetic analysis, and led to the development of the laser. Einstein's derivation inspired further developments in the quantum treatment of light, which led to the statistical interpretation of quantum mechanics.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AStimulatedemission.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/DLP_Home_Theatre_Projector.jpg/500px-DLP_Home_Theatre_Projector.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/DLP_Home_Theatre_Projector.jpg/1280px-DLP_Home_Theatre_Projector.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_ca